// C7 conformance corpus (#95): the String->Option<u64> parser, USED. The oracle
// (parse_u64.cert.json) is hand-derived (R-CHAR-3) from .design/basis/09-option-result.md
// AC-4 + 07-strings.md REQ-9. The deliverable is that a caller given a valid, in-range
// digit string PROVES it gets Some with the round-trip value (the strengthened parse_u64
// contract: all_digits(s) && in-range ==> result is Some && parse_be(s) == the payload).
// The loud None arms (non-digit / overflow / empty) are the handled-or-loud teeth.

// A valid digit string in range parses to Some of its big-endian value — proven (not
// runtime-checked). `ens result is Some` discharges from the req via parse_u64's
// strengthened contract; the round-trip `Some(v) => parse_be(s) == v` follows. A None
// mutant violates `result is Some` given the req -> mutation-killable -> L3.
fn parse_valid(s: String) -> Option<u64>
  req all_digits(s) && s.len() >= 1 && parse_be(s) <= 18446744073709551615
  ens result is Some
  ens match result { Some(v) => parse_be(s) == v, None => true }
  fx  pure
{
  parse_u64(s)
}

// A non-digit string yields the loud None arm (handled-or-loud): the parser refuses,
// it does not return a wrong value. `!all_digits(s) ==> result is None` is the
// contractual guarantee that a malformed input is REFUSED, proven.
fn parse_rejects_nondigit(s: String) -> Option<u64>
  req s.len() >= 1 && !all_digits(s)
  ens match result { Some(v) => false, None => true }
  fx  pure
{
  parse_u64(s)
}
